Here's the approach:
1. Traverse through the input string while keeping track of the current depth of parentheses.
2. Whenever the depth returns to zero after increasing from a previous point, it indicates the end of a parenthetical group.
3. Use an array to collect these groups as they are identified during traversal.

The solution involves maintaining a counter for the current depth and using a list to accumulate the results. When the depth reaches zero again after being non-zero, we know we've completed a group.

```dart
import 'dart:collection';

List<String> separateParenGroups(String parenString) {
  final List<String> result = [];
  int depth = 0;
  String currentGroup = '';

  for (int i = 0; i < parenString.length; i++) {
    final char = parenString[i];

    if (char == '(') {
      depth++;
    } else if (char == ')') {
      depth--;
    }

    currentGroup += char;

    // Check if we have closed all open parenthesis
    if (depth == 0 && i != parenString.length - 1) {
      result.add(currentGroup);
      currentGroup = '';
    } else if (i == parenString.length - 1 && depth == 0) {
      result.add(currentGroup);
    }
  }

  return result;
}
```

This code processes each character in the input string, tracks the depth of nested parentheses, and collects complete groups when the depth returns to zero. The resulting list contains each separated parenthetical group as a string element.

